---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import catalog from "../../data/provider-catalog.json";

const basePath = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
const providers = catalog.providers;
const modelsDevCount = providers.filter(
  (provider) => provider.source === "models.dev",
).length;
const modelsDevCommit = catalog.source.commit;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="AI providers"
  description="The provider architecture and current integration status for Veasel Code."
>
  <section class="doc-page">
    <p class="kicker">FIELD GUIDE / PROVIDERS</p>
    <h1>
      Bring the model
      <br />
      <em>you want to work with.</em>
    </h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Veasel Code keeps model connections behind a small V provider interface.
      Choose from {providers.length} presets across four wire protocols and use
      the same persistent session workflow. Presets are not independent native
      SDK integrations: {modelsDevCount} entries from the pinned Models.dev
      catalog share Veasel's OpenAI-compatible Chat Completions adapter.
    </p>
    <div class="doc-panel">
      <h2>Available in the current source build</h2>
      <p>
        The V backend streams bounded responses, keeps credentials server-side,
        and stores each successful user/assistant exchange in the session
        database. Provider-native tool calls can request bounded, workspace
        listing, file reads, literal search, and a single-file edit proposal. A
        proposal is a saved diff; it does not modify the workspace until a
        person opens and approves it in the TUI. Requested file content and tool
        results go back to the configured provider. Model responses stream
        through durable session events. A model can also propose a shell
        command; the TUI requires opening the exact command, working directory,
        and timeout before approval. Approved commands may use a platform
        sandbox when available. Shell output and requested file content are
        returned to the configured provider.
      </p>
      <div
        class="provider-table"
        role="table"
        aria-label="Provider implementation status"
      >
        <div class="provider-table-row provider-table-head" role="row">
          <b role="columnheader">Provider</b>
          <b role="columnheader">Status</b>
          <b role="columnheader">Notes</b>
        </div>
        <div class="provider-table-row" role="row">
          <b role="cell">OpenAI-compatible</b>
          <span role="cell">Available</span>
          <span role="cell">
            Default adapter; custom HTTPS or loopback endpoint
          </span>
        </div>
        <div class="provider-table-row" role="row">
          <b role="cell">OpenAI Responses</b>
          <span role="cell">Available</span>
          <span role="cell">
            Responses API; reasoning only for supported models
          </span>
        </div>
        <div class="provider-table-row" role="row">
          <b role="cell">Anthropic</b>
          <span role="cell">Available</span>
          <span role="cell">Native Messages API</span>
        </div>
        <div class="provider-table-row" role="row">
          <b role="cell">Gemini</b>
          <span role="cell">Available</span>
          <span role="cell">Native generateContent API</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="doc-panel">
      <h2>Provider presets</h2>
      <p>
        Anthropic and Gemini use their native HTTP APIs. Most remaining presets
        use OpenAI Chat Completions. OpenAI Responses and Meta Model API use the
        Responses API; the OpenAI Responses preset targets GPT-5 and Codex
        models, while the original OpenAI Chat Completions preset remains
        available. Provider-specific features are not implied. Choose an entry
        in Settings with <kbd>s</kbd>
        on the home screen or <code>/models</code> in a session. Search this
        catalog by provider, ID, endpoint, or credential variable.
      </p>
      <h3>OpenAI Responses models</h3>
      <p>
        Choose <code>OpenAI Responses</code> to discover models from your OpenAI
        account and use GPT-5 or Codex models with the Responses API. Veasel
        requests high reasoning effort and encrypted reasoning replay only for
        model families that support it; other models use the same Responses
        transport without reasoning options. The existing OpenAI Chat
        Completions preset remains available for models or endpoints that need
        that protocol.
      </p>
      <h3>Meta Muse models</h3>
      <p>
        Veasel includes a native OpenAI Responses adapter for Meta's Model API.
        Configure provider <code>meta</code>, model <code>muse-spark-1.3</code>
        or <code>muse-wonder-1.0</code>, and the
        <code>MODEL_API_KEY</code> credential. Spark 1.3 is Meta's recommended
        coding model; Muse Wonder is also available on the Model API. Muse
        Wonder Full is a Muse Code subscription model and is not exposed by this
        API. Veasel currently uses high reasoning effort, streams text and tool
        calls, and replays encrypted reasoning within a tool loop. Its request
        and output limits are lower than the models' advertised context window,
        so the full one-million-token context is not currently usable.
      </p>
      <pre><code>export VEASEL_MODEL_PROVIDER=meta
export VEASEL_MODEL=muse-spark-1.3
export MODEL_API_KEY=your-meta-model-api-key
veasel</code></pre>
      <p>
        See Meta's{" "}
        <a href="https://dev.meta.ai/docs/coding-agents">coding-agent setup</a>,
        <a href="https://dev.meta.ai/docs/models">model list</a>, and
        <a href="https://dev.meta.ai/docs/protocols/responses">Responses API</a>
        documentation for account access and API behavior.
      </p>
      <p>
        {modelsDevCount} compatible endpoint presets are sourced from the
        MIT-licensed{" "}
        <a
          href={`https://github.com/anomalyco/models.dev/tree/${modelsDevCommit}`}
        >
          Models.dev snapshot {modelsDevCommit.slice(0, 12)}
        </a>
        , which OpenCode also uses for its provider catalog. They share the same
        Veasel transport and do not represent independent SDKs or feature
        parity. The catalog is compiled into Veasel; releases do not fetch
        provider metadata at runtime. For the pinned source and selection rules,
        see the{" "}
        <a href="https://github.com/veasel-labs/veasel/blob/main/docs/PROVIDERS.md">
          Veasel provider reference
        </a>
        .
      </p>
      <label class="provider-filter" for="provider-filter">
        <span>Filter providers</span>
        <input
          id="provider-filter"
          type="search"
          placeholder="Name, ID, endpoint, or environment variable"
          autocomplete="off"
          aria-controls="provider-catalog-rows"
        />
      </label>
      <p id="provider-count" class="provider-count" aria-live="polite">
        Showing {providers.length} of {providers.length} provider presets.
      </p>
      <div
        class="provider-catalog-wrap"
        role="region"
        aria-label="Provider catalog"
        tabindex="0"
      >
        <table class="provider-catalog">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Provider</th>
              <th scope="col">Transport</th>
              <th scope="col">Default endpoint</th>
              <th scope="col">Credential</th>
              <th scope="col">Reference</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody id="provider-catalog-rows">
            {providers.map((provider) => (
              <tr
                data-provider-row
                data-search={`${provider.name} ${provider.id} ${provider.protocol} ${provider.api} ${provider.environment_key}`.toLocaleLowerCase()}
              >
                <th scope="row">
                  <span>{provider.name}</span>
                  <small>{provider.id}</small>
                </th>
                <td>
                  {provider.protocol === "openai-compatible"
                    ? "OpenAI-compatible"
                    : provider.protocol}
                </td>
                <td>
                  <code>{provider.api}</code>
                </td>
                <td>
                  {provider.requires_api_key
                    ? provider.environment_key
                    : "Not required by preset"}
                </td>
                <td>
                  {provider.documentation_url ? (
                    <a
                      href={provider.documentation_url}
                      target="_blank"
                      rel="noreferrer"
                    >
                      Provider docs
                    </a>
                  ) : (
                    <a href="https://github.com/veasel-labs/veasel/blob/main/docs/PROVIDERS.md">
                      Veasel catalog
                    </a>
                  )}
                </td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <h3>Model selection</h3>
      <p>
        Settings requests the provider's model list before the profile is saved.
        It uses the key typed in this session, a saved profile, or the
        provider's environment variable. This is a model-list request, not a
        generation request. If the endpoint does not support listing or the
        request fails, enter a model ID manually. Choosing a model saves the
        profile. The output-token budget is already filled in;
        <kbd>Ctrl+O</kbd> changes it, from 16 to 131,072 tokens. 0 selects
        Veasel's 4,096-token default, except Meta, which omits{" "}
        <code>max_output_tokens</code>. A chat message can contain up to 1 MiB
        (1,048,576 bytes) of text. Veasel does not yet use Muse's full
        advertised 1,048,576-token context window. API keys are never returned
        by the settings API. See the{" "}
        <a href={`${basePath}/docs/api/`}>model-list endpoint</a> for its
        contract.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="doc-panel">
      <h2>Choose a provider in Veasel</h2>
      <p>
        Press <kbd>s</kbd> on the home screen or enter <code>/models</code> in
        an open session. Search the catalog and choose a provider. A known
        endpoint is filled in; <kbd>Ctrl+U</kbd> replaces it. Enter a key only
        when the provider requires one. Press <kbd>Ctrl+O</kbd> to change the
        filled-in output-token budget. Veasel keeps a separate profile for each
        provider. Leave the key blank to keep the saved value, or enter{" "}
        <code>!clear</code> to remove it. Keys are never shown again in the TUI
        or returned by the API.
      </p>
      <p>
        Profiles live in <code>$VEASEL_DATA_DIR/model-settings.json</code>; on
        POSIX, the file is restricted to the current user. Values are stored in
        plaintext on disk, so use environment variables when local policy
        requires secrets to be managed by an OS keychain.
      </p>
      <h3>Headless configuration</h3>
      <pre><code>export VEASEL_MODEL_PROVIDER=anthropic
export VEASEL_MODEL=your-model-name
export ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=your-api-key
veasel</code></pre>
      <p>
        Use <code>OPENAI_API_KEY</code> with
        <code>openai-compatible</code>, or <code>GEMINI_API_KEY</code> with
        <code>gemini</code>. Set <code>VEASEL_MODEL_BASE_URL</code> for a
        compatible endpoint; custom endpoints require HTTPS or loopback HTTP.
        Environment configuration remains available for scripts and automation.
      </p>
    </div>
    <p class="callout">
      <strong>Today:</strong> configured sessions can stream responses, inspect
      the selected workspace through bounded tools, propose a single-file edit
      for review, and restore recent history. Applying a proposal requires
      opening its diff and explicitly approving it in the TUI. Proposed shell
      commands require the same exact review and approval before running, and
      may wrap in a platform sandbox when available. File excerpts and tool
      output are sent to your configured provider. See the
      <a href="https://github.com/veasel-labs/veasel/blob/main/docs/IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md">
        implementation plan
      </a>
      for current progress.
    </p>
    <a class="text-link" href={`${basePath}/docs/architecture/`}>
      See the architecture <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
    </a>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>

<script>
  const providerFilter =
    document.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>("#provider-filter");
  const providerCount = document.querySelector("#provider-count");
  const providerRows = Array.from(
    document.querySelectorAll<HTMLTableRowElement>("[data-provider-row]"),
  );

  providerFilter?.addEventListener("input", () => {
    const query = providerFilter.value.trim().toLocaleLowerCase();
    let visible = 0;
    for (const row of providerRows) {
      const matches = row.dataset.search?.includes(query) ?? false;
      row.hidden = !matches;
      if (matches) visible += 1;
    }
    if (providerCount) {
      providerCount.textContent = `Showing ${visible} of ${providerRows.length} provider presets.`;
    }
  });
</script>

<style>
  .provider-filter {
    display: grid;
    gap: 8px;
    margin-top: 20px;
    color: var(--muted);
    font: 0.68rem var(--mono);
  }
  .provider-filter input {
    width: 100%;
    min-height: 46px;
    padding: 10px 13px;
    color: var(--ink);
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: 7px;
    font: 0.76rem var(--body);
  }
  .provider-filter input:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--green);
    outline-offset: 2px;
  }
  .provider-count {
    color: var(--muted);
    font: 0.62rem var(--mono);
  }
  .provider-catalog-wrap {
    overflow: auto;
    max-height: 68vh;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: 8px;
    scrollbar-color: var(--line) transparent;
  }
  .provider-catalog-wrap:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--green);
    outline-offset: 3px;
  }
  .provider-catalog {
    width: 100%;
    min-width: 920px;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    text-align: left;
    font-size: 0.68rem;
  }
  .provider-catalog th,
  .provider-catalog td {
    padding: 11px 12px;
    vertical-align: top;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }
  .provider-catalog thead {
    position: sticky;
    z-index: 1;
    top: 0;
    color: var(--muted);
    background: var(--surface);
    font: 0.56rem var(--mono);
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
  }
  .provider-catalog tbody th {
    min-width: 145px;
    font-weight: 650;
  }
  .provider-catalog tbody th small {
    display: block;
    margin-top: 4px;
    color: var(--muted);
    font: 0.56rem var(--mono);
  }
  .provider-catalog td {
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  .provider-catalog code {
    color: var(--green-dark);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
    font: 0.57rem var(--mono);
  }
  .provider-catalog a {
    color: var(--green-dark);
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
  }
  .provider-catalog tr[hidden] {
    display: none;
  }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .provider-catalog-wrap {
      scroll-behavior: auto;
    }
  }
</style>
